  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and a few thunderstorms Saturday into Sunday. \nGusty post frontal winds on Monday. Another system approaches \nmidweek to bring another chance for rain. \n\nA cold front, with stronger upper level support with a deepening \ntrough approaches the area Saturday. Pre-frontal showers and a few \nmay develop mid to late afternoon Saturday, as SBCAPE climbs into \nthe 500 to 1000 J/kg ahead of the front with plenty of daytime \nheating. However, stronger lift and greater bulk shear come with the \nfront, so thinking the majority of the rain will come during the \novernight hours and continue into Sunday. Timing of frontal passage \nis not very conducive to convective development, but could see some \nstorms track into the area from the west with the front. Shower and \nstorm activity will weaken as it crosses the mountains, and so not \nexpecting as much rainfall in the east as over the mountains. \nRainfall amounts have trended slightly lower in the Greenbrier \nValley, between two and four tenths of an inch of rain, and slightly \nhigher in the Mountain Empire region of VA, between a quarter to \nhalf an inch of rain. Still looks to be between a tenth and a \nquarter of an inch of rain over the New River and Roanoke Valleys, \nand then less than a tenth of an inch east of the Blue Ridge. This \nmeager amount of rainfall will not help improve the ongoing severe \ndrought in central VA and NC. With the colder air filtering in \nbehind the frontal passage, may see some snow showers in the higher \nelevation locations of southeast WV Sunday night, though warm ground \ntemperatures will likely preclude any accumulations.\n\nLarge surface high pressure arrives behind the front to start the \nwork week, until another frontal systems starts to drop southward \ntowards the area during the Wednesday/Thursday timeframe. Rebounding \ntemperatures and increasing moisture will lead to scattered showers \nand possible thunderstorms with the front nearby, but initial \namounts look to be limited at this time. Without much wetting \nrainfall expected east of the Blue Ridge through the next seven \ndays, can anticipate drought conditions to remain or worsen for \nthose areas, and will likely have to monitor for fire weather \nconcerns Monday with gusty northwesterly winds. See the Fire Weather \nSection for more details on fire danger through the week.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Post frontal airmass will bring more seasonal\ntemperatures, with a couple chances for frost/freeze Sunday\nnight and Monday night.\n\nUpper level trough behind the cold front passage Sunday, will be\nquasi-stationary over the eastern Conus through early next week.\nCold air advection behind the cold front Sunday night into\nMonday morning will allow temperatures to fall into the low to\nmid 30s for areas west of the Blue Ridge. This may lead to some\ncrop damage across the area for those that have started growing.\nWinds behind the front should remain elevated, and RH values\ndon't look to increase into the 80-100% range, so the threat for\nfrost looks minimal Sunday night at this time. While frost looks\nminimal, the cold temperatures may still damage sensitive\nplants. \n\nAs the upper level trough remains over the region, a \nsurface high pressure system looks to settle over the Mid- \nAtlantic states Monday night into Tuesday. This will allow \ntemperatures to be even colder, with values near or below \nfreezing areawide Monday night into Tuesday. With high pressure\noverhead, winds look to be near calm across the region\novernight. RH values overnight will only increase some, with\nlocalized values approaching near 80% in some regions; however,\nthe dry airmass behind the front will keep RH's quite low. With\ncalm winds areawide, and some localized areas of higher RH\npossible, there will likely be some localized frost across the\nregion. Folks that have planted crops or have started gardens\nwill want to take precautions with widespread temperatures near\nor below freezing expected.",
